title: Create New Deal
description: >-
  Create a new deal in your sales pipeline and track its progress from lead to
  closure. Assign owners, set deal value, add contacts, and manage every stage
  of your sales process in one place.
sidebar:
  order: 3
---
The **Create New Deal** feature allows users to add a new sales opportunity into a pipeline and begin tracking its lifecycle from the earliest stage to closure. A deal represents a potential revenue-generating interaction with a lead, contact, or company.

This section explains each field, behavior, and best practice in detail.

## Overview

Clicking **+ Add Deal** opens a side panel with three parts:

- A **Deal Name** and **Link this deal to** section, always visible at the top
- A collapsible **Deal Details** section (stage, priority, amount, currency, tags)
- A collapsible **Additional** section (description, documents, custom fields)

Both collapsible sections start closed, so a new deal can be created with just a name, an owner, and a linked client/lead/company — everything else is optional until you expand it.

Once created, the deal becomes visible in the pipeline board under the selected stage and can be managed through its lifecycle.

*Note: the pipeline list on the left of the Sales Pipeline screen can be collapsed to a slim icon rail to give the board more room — this doesn't affect deal creation, it only changes how you switch between pipelines.*

## Field-Level Breakdown

### A. Deal Name

**Type:** Text input (required)\
**Purpose:** Identifies the deal uniquely within the pipeline.

**Guidelines:**

- Use clear, descriptive naming
- Recommended format:\
  `Client Name – Service/Product – Month`

**Example:**

- “Acme Corp – Website Redesign – May”

### B. Link this deal to

**Type:** Tab selector — **Client**, **Lead**, or **Company** (required, choose exactly one)\
**Purpose:** Connects the deal to the person or organization it's being pursued with.

**Behavior:**

- Switching tabs clears whatever was selected on the other tabs, so a deal is only ever linked to one client, one lead, or one company at a time
- **Client:** picks from existing Contacts
- **Lead:** picks from existing Leads
- **Company:** picks from existing Company accounts, useful for B2B deals not yet tied to one specific contact
- At least one of the three must be selected before the deal can be created

### C. Owner & Assignee

**Type:** Team member selection (required)\
**Purpose:** Assigns responsibility for managing the deal.

**Behavior:**

- The selected user becomes the deal owner
- Appears in reporting and performance tracking

### D. Stage

**Type:** Dropdown (required, inside **Deal Details**)\
**Default:** The stage of the pipeline column you clicked **+ Add Deal** from, or the pipeline's first stage if none was pre-selected

**Purpose:** Defines the current position of the deal in the pipeline.

**Behavior:**

- Options are the real stages configured for the current pipeline
- Determines where the deal appears on the pipeline board
- Can be changed later via drag-and-drop or edit

### E. Priority

**Type:** Dropdown (inside **Deal Details**)\
Options:

- Low (default)
- Medium
- High
- Urgent

**Purpose:** Helps prioritize deal handling.

### F. Lead Type

**Type:** Dropdown (inside **Deal Details**)\
Options:

- Cold
- Warm
- Hot

**Purpose:** Indicates engagement level. Only shown when the deal is linked to a **Lead** (section B) — it doesn't apply to Client- or Company-linked deals.

### G. Deal Status

**Type:** Dropdown (inside **Deal Details**)\
Options:

- Open (default)
- Negotiation
- Won
- Lost

**Purpose:** Tracks where the deal stands independently of its pipeline stage.

### H. Deal Amount

**Type:** Numeric input (required, inside **Deal Details**)\
**Purpose:** Defines the monetary value of the deal.

**Behavior:**

- Used for revenue forecasting and pipeline column totals
- Can be updated as negotiations progress

### I. Closing Date

**Type:** Date picker (inside **Deal Details**)\
**Purpose:** Expected deal completion date.

**Behavior:**

- Used for forecasting
- Helps identify delays or overdue deals

### J. Currency

**Type:** Dropdown (inside **Deal Details**)\
**Default:** Your workspace's configured business currency

**Purpose:** Specifies the currency for the deal value.

**Behavior:**

- Can be overridden per deal
- Ensures accurate financial reporting across the pipeline

### K. Tags

**Type:** Multi-select (inside **Deal Details**)\
**Purpose:** Categorizes deals for filtering and segmentation.

**Behavior:**

- Pick from existing tags or type a new one to create it on the fly

### L. Description

**Type:** Rich text editor (inside **Additional**)\
**Purpose:** Stores additional deal context — requirements, client expectations, internal notes.

**Behavior:**

- Includes an AI-assist option to help draft the description

### M. Select Documents

**Type:** Multi-select (inside **Additional**)\
**Purpose:** Attach existing workspace documents such as proposals, contracts, or quotations to the deal.

### N. Add Custom Field

**Type:** Dynamic input, opened from the **+ Add Custom Field** button in the panel footer\
**Purpose:** Extend deal data beyond the default fields (e.g. Industry, Deal Source, Competitor, Contract Duration).

## Actions

### A. Create Deal

- Validates all required fields
- Saves deal into the selected pipeline
- Assigns it to the chosen stage
- Triggers automation (if configured)

### B. Cancel

- Discards all inputs
- No data is saved

## System Behavior After Creation

Once a deal is created:

- It appears in the pipeline board under the selected stage
- It becomes accessible via the Deal Details page
- Activity tracking begins (timeline logging)

## Validation Rules

- Required fields must be completed:
  - Deal Name
  - A linked Client, Lead, or Company (exactly one)
  - Owner & Assignee
  - Stage
  - Deal Amount
- Invalid or missing data will prevent submission
- Numeric fields must accept valid numbers only

## Best Practices

- Use consistent naming conventions for deals
- Assign deals immediately to avoid ownership gaps
- Set realistic closing dates for accurate forecasting
- Update deal amount as negotiations evolve
- Use tags for better filtering and reporting

## Example Deal Creation Flow

1. User clicks **+ Add Deal**
1. Enters:
   - Deal Name: “ABC Ltd – Mobile App Development”
   - Link this deal to: **Client** → ABC Ltd
   - Owner & Assignee: John
1. Expands **Deal Details** and sets:
   - Stage: (defaults to the column clicked, or first stage)
   - Priority: High
   - Deal Amount: $5,000
1. Expands **Additional**, adds a description and attaches a proposal document
1. Clicks **Create Deal**

→ Deal is added to the pipeline and visible to the team
